[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc12,r12
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: LAN IP addressing/subnet mask on the PC12 access segment and the gateway’s matching interface; plus the EIGRP advertisement on r12. Mechanism: Both PC12 and its gateway r12.eth1 are configured with a /8 mask (255.0.0.0) for 120.1.1.x. This makes the entire 120.0.0.0/8 appear “on‑link” to both nodes. Traffic to any remote 120.x.x.x host gets ARP-resolved on the local wire, fails, and stalls for ARP timeout seconds before applications fall back to other IPs/paths—perceived as brief random “drops.” Localization: PC12 eth0 shows inet 120.1.1.10/8 and default via 120.1.1.1; r12 eth1 shows inet 120.1.1.1/8. From PC12, ip route get 120.2.2.2 returns “dev eth0 src 120.1.1.10,” and ping 120.2.2.2 fails (100% loss). From r12, ip route get 120.2.2.2 returns “dev eth1 src 120.1.1.1,” and ping fails—proving both ends wrongly consider 120/8 on-link. Root cause: Mis-subnetting—PC12 and r12.eth1 configured with 120.1.1.x/8 instead of the intended /24; r12’s EIGRP also advertises 120.0.0.0/8, compounding the issue. Corrective fix: Change PC12 eth0 to 120.1.1.10/24 and r12.eth1 to 120.1.1.1/24; update FRR on r12 to advertise 120.1.1.0/24 (replace “network 120.0.0.0/8” with “network 120.1.1.0/24”); reload FRR; flush ARP/neighbor caches. This will stop on-link blackholing and eliminate the intermittent stalls. === END ===